## Building and Flying Together

The Aeronautical and Flight Systems Division is where students passionate about airplanes, drones, and flight gather to design, build, and test flying models.

Whether you have always wanted to build your own radio-controlled (RC) airplane or want to understand how autonomous drones fly waypoints on a map, our division is the place to get your hands dirty with foam, motors, and wings.

## What We Do in Our Club

### 1. Model Aircraft and Foam Glider Building
We believe the best way to understand aerodynamics is to build simple airplanes that actually fly:
- Cutting and shaping foam board and balsa wood to make lightweight wings and fuselages
- Understanding why airfoils generate lift and how center of gravity balances a plane
- Installing small brushless electric motors, propellers, and servos to control rudders and elevators

### 2. Custom Drones and Quadcopters
Members learn how multirotor drones work by assembling and configuring them:
- Soldering electronic speed controllers (ESCs), power distribution boards, and flight controllers
- Calibrating gyroscopes and accelerometers for stable hovering
- Practicing safe flying in open university grounds using manual and stabilized flight modes

### 3. Flight Autopilot and Telemetry
For students interested in software and electronics:
- Configuring open-source autopilot software like ArduPilot and INAV on flight controllers
- Setting up GPS receivers to enable features like Return-to-Home and autonomous waypoint routes
- Monitoring battery voltage, speed, and altitude live on a ground laptop via radio telemetry

## What You Get as a Student Member

- Hands-On Crafting Skills: Learn practical fabrication with foam board, adhesives, soldering irons, and RC electronics.
- Exciting Flight Days: Head out to open campus fields on weekends to test fly new builds and cheer on teammates.
- Supportive Community: Share tips, fix broken propellers together, and collaborate on creative aircraft designs.
